 "Training side": "",
 "Fine-tuning on many examples of (tools, request) → correct call teaches <0>when</0> to call a tool and the <1>format</1>. Reinforcement learning on whether calls succeed can sharpen it (<2>SFT</2>).": "",
 "Decoding side": "",
 "A grammar / JSON-Schema compiler tracks which tokens may legally come next and masks all others. Some providers expose this as a \"strict\" tool or structured-output option; availability varies.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>Masking demo</0>": "",
 "A tool call is <0>just tokens</0>. The model has no special \"JSON mode\" in its architecture: it predicts the next token like always.": "",
 "Two things make it reliable: <0>training</0> on many tool-call examples, and optionally <1>constrained decoding</1>, which masks tokens that would break the grammar or schema.": "",
 "Constrained decoding sets the logits of invalid tokens to −∞ before softmax, so they get probability exactly {#}.": "",
 "It can guarantee syntax and schema shape, not that the <0>values</0> are right (the city can be valid JSON and still wrong).": "",
